From: Jesús <jesus33050@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] gba: let a game set the multiplayer cable transfer time

The Legend of Zelda: Four Swords never leaves its "Linking with other
systems" screen on a two-GBA cable. When the lobby switches from polled to
interrupt-driven transfers, the parent's link init runs about 5,450 CPU
cycles after the last polled transfer started, and only enables the timer
IRQ that drives later transfers if SIOCNT is no longer busy. With the
reference 5,755-cycle transfer (36 bits plus mGBA's ~512-cycle timeout) the
transfer is still busy, so neither GBA ever transfers again. 5,450 cycles
or less lets it through; 5,500 does not.

Whether real hardware is faster (GBATEK does not give the timeout length)
or the recompiled CPU spends too few cycles on that path is still open, so
this does not change the defaults. It adds
GbaLinkHub::set_multiplayer_transfer_cycles(baud, participants, cycles):
a per-process override a game sets before any session starts. Every netplay
peer runs the same build, so the value stays deterministic. 0 restores the
default. multiplayer_transfer_cycles() reads the effective value.

Used by FourSwordsRecomp with 5,243 cycles (the 36 bits alone at
115,200 bps) for two GBAs.

---
diff --git a/src/gba/gba_link_hub.cpp b/src/gba/gba_link_hub.cpp
index c5687d2..b38ce29 100644
--- a/src/gba/gba_link_hub.cpp
+++ b/src/gba/gba_link_hub.cpp
@@ -15,6 +15,22 @@ constexpr std::uint32_t kTransferCycles[4][3] = {
     {63427, 94884, 125829}, {16241, 24104, 31457},
     {10998, 16241, 20972}, {5755, 8376, 10486}
 };
+// Per-process game overrides (0 = use kTransferCycles).
+std::uint32_t g_transfer_override[4][3] = {};
+}
+
+void GbaLinkHub::set_multiplayer_transfer_cycles(unsigned baud, std::size_t participants,
+                                                 std::uint32_t cycles) {
+    if (baud > 3 || participants < 2 || participants > kCablePorts)
+        throw std::invalid_argument("multiplayer transfer timing: baud 0-3, 2-4 participants");
+    g_transfer_override[baud][participants - 2] = cycles;
+}
+
+std::uint32_t GbaLinkHub::multiplayer_transfer_cycles(unsigned baud, std::size_t participants) {
+    if (baud > 3 || participants < 2 || participants > kCablePorts)
+        throw std::invalid_argument("multiplayer transfer timing: baud 0-3, 2-4 participants");
+    const auto custom = g_transfer_override[baud][participants - 2];
+    return custom ? custom : kTransferCycles[baud][participants - 2];
 }
 
 GbaLinkHub::GbaLinkHub(std::size_t port_count) : count_(port_count) {
@@ -151,7 +167,7 @@ void GbaLinkHub::start_normal(std::size_t port) {
 }
 void GbaLinkHub::start() {
     if (!ready()) return;
-    const auto duration = kTransferCycles[ports_[0].control & 3][count_ - 2];
+    const auto duration = multiplayer_transfer_cycles(ports_[0].control & 3, count_);
     if (ports_[0].cycle > std::numeric_limits<std::uint64_t>::max() - duration)
         throw std::overflow_error("GBA cable cycle overflow");
     transfer_.fill(0xffff);
diff --git a/src/gba/gba_link_hub.h b/src/gba/gba_link_hub.h
index 5b9c02a..938b22e 100644
--- a/src/gba/gba_link_hub.h
+++ b/src/gba/gba_link_hub.h
@@ -22,6 +22,16 @@ public:
     GbaLinkHub(const GbaLinkHub&) = delete;
     GbaLinkHub& operator=(const GbaLinkHub&) = delete;
 
+    // Multiplayer transfer duration in CPU cycles for a baud setting (SIOCNT
+    // bits 0-1) and a cable of 2-4 participants. The defaults are the mGBA
+    // reference timings; a game may replace one before any session starts,
+    // when its own handshake proves them too long for this runtime (every
+    // netplay peer runs the same build, so the value stays deterministic).
+    // 0 restores the default. Not part of snapshots.
+    static void set_multiplayer_transfer_cycles(unsigned baud, std::size_t participants,
+                                                std::uint32_t cycles);
+    static std::uint32_t multiplayer_transfer_cycles(unsigned baud, std::size_t participants);
+
     GbaSerialDevice& endpoint(std::size_t port);
     std::size_t port_count() const { return count_; }
     std::uint64_t cycle(std::size_t port) const;
